p1 = [
 "Long before taps and pipelines reached our homes, the people of India's dry regions had found an ingenious way to store water: the stepwell. Known as a baoli in the north and a vav in Gujarat, a stepwell is a deep well with flights of steps leading down to the water. Hundreds of them were built across Rajasthan, Gujarat and neighbouring states over many centuries. Rani ki Vav at Patan, honoured as a World Heritage Site in 2014, and the Chand Baori at Abhaneri, with its dizzying pattern of steps, are two of the finest examples.",
 "The steps were no mere decoration. In regions where rainfall is scarce and uncertain, the level of water in a well changes with the seasons. A person could walk down the steps to reach the water when it was low and need only a few steps when the monsoon had filled the well. The steps also allowed people, cattle and travelling merchants to use the water directly without ropes and buckets. Pillared pavilions and shaded corridors beside the water offered travellers a cool place to rest in the blazing summer.",
 "Stepwells were also centres of community life. Women gathered there each day to draw water and exchange news, festivals were celebrated beside them, and small shrines were often built at their edges. Skilled craftsmen carved gods, dancers and patterns on the walls, which shows that the builders cared about beauty as much as usefulness. At the same time, the wells played a quiet environmental role. By collecting monsoon run-off and letting it sink slowly into the ground, they helped to recharge groundwater.",
 "This careful system declined under changing conditions. During the colonial period, piped water was introduced in many towns and stepwells came to be regarded as unhygienic. After independence, hand pumps and tube wells replaced them even in villages. Without regular use, the wells were neglected; some were filled with garbage and rubble, while others dried up as the groundwater fell. Over time, many people forgot that these structures had ever been the lifeline of entire villages, and the knowledge of how to maintain them slowly disappeared along with the craftsmen who built them.",
 "In recent years, however, local communities, heritage experts and conservation agencies have begun to clean and repair these monuments. Some restored stepwells now supply water again and help to collect rainwater in nearby fields. These efforts remind us that sustainable answers to scarcity often lie in local knowledge. By valuing our stepwells, we protect an inheritance of art, engineering and wisdom that our water-stressed age badly needs.",
]
